Literature for cycle 2 is in `runs/Absolute_2026-09-28_02/sota/cycle-002/literature-8.json`. The query is the measured latency of sending stage 2 only the pallet `select_detection` already chooses, and of putting both the sent index and the deferred indices on `PalletPoseStamped`.

- **Stage 2 is the cost that scales.** Classic chamfer is 17.59 µs per hypothesis on a 1,610-point template (Liu et al., CVPR 2010). This node's free search is 28,306 hypotheses on a 182-point contour, and the prior grids bring that to 45,692 when the free cost is above about 0.00623 m. Scaling their per-hypothesis time by contour length is a serial envelope of about 56 ms, or about 91 ms if the prior grids also run. The loop is OpenMP and evaluates every cell, so that envelope is not a clock of this node. A deferred index does not enter the search.
- **A learned pose has the same per-object multiplier.** CosyPose spent 190 ms posing every candidate in a 4-view, 6-detection scene. FoundationPose measured 1.3 s per object. One precise distance image at 640×480 is 0.788 ms; deferred candidates skip both that image and the grid.
- **The two index lists are a small add on the pose the controller already reads.** ROS 2 latency was the same at 128 B, 1 KB, and 10 KB, and Fast DDS serialization of a pose is about 15 ns. A second small-message hop measured 113 µs P90 at 100 Hz. An array of elements that each contain a `Time` is the slow shape: 5,000 of those could not hold 1,000 Hz. One `int32` and one `int32[]` stay on the primitive side. The failure path adds those same lists to the only diagnostic it publishes. The two early returns publish nothing.
